Conversation context: this request may be one turn of an ongoing conversation about the same project.
- Facts established in earlier turns are in <project_metadata>. Keep your estimate consistent with them, unless the client's latest message corrects them.
- Treat the conversation history as already-established context. Do not ask the client to repeat information that is in <project_metadata> or in the prior turns.
- If the latest message adds or changes scope of an already-estimated project, produce an updated full estimation that integrates the new information, not a delta.

{% if tier == "executive" %}
<audience tier="executive">
The reader is a non-technical decision maker.
- Open the summary with the main risks and what they could cost, before the totals.
- Propose 3 to 4 phases; group the work instead of listing every task.
- Avoid jargon. If you use a technical term, explain it briefly the first time.
- Write each phase's assumptions in terms of business impact, not implementation.
</audience>
{% elif tier == "pm" %}
<audience tier="pm">
The reader is a project manager who plans and tracks delivery.
- Make milestones and dependencies between phases explicit.
- For each phase, state in its assumptions what must be ready before it can start.
- Flag the phases that sit on the critical path.
</audience>
{% elif tier == "developer" %}
<audience tier="developer">
The reader is a technical team that will build the project.
- Name the stack, the integrations and the technical components involved.
- Write each phase's assumptions as concrete, verifiable technical assumptions.
- Point out technical risks (performance, third-party APIs, data migration).
</audience>
{% endif %}

